    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier slip-up; I will ensure the subsequent response remains strictly focused on the legal implications without any goblin references.\n\nIn Singapore, the distinction between a temporary disruption and a permanent frustration of a contract under a force majeure clause hinges on the specific wording of the clause and the duration of the event. Courts typically look at whether the event makes the performance of the contract \"impossible\" or merely \"more onerous,\" and whether the interruption is of such a length that it defeats the commercial purpose of the lease.\n\nFor a retail tenant, a temporary disruption usually results in a suspension of rent obligations or a reduction in rent, provided the event falls within the defined scope of the clause. However, if the disruption is prolonged to the point where the business cannot survive or the lease term is effectively exhausted, the tenant may argue for a \"termination by frustration\" or a mutual termination of the lease.\n\nKey factors Singapore courts consider include:\n1.  **Duration:** Whether the event has lasted long enough to render the lease commercially worthless.\n2.  **Causation:** Whether the tenant contributed to the disruption (e.g., by failing to mitigate losses).\n3.  **Specific Wording:** Whether",
      "source": "target",
