    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Catch-all language in a force majeure clause is generally a double-edged sword for tenants in Singapore, often leaning towards hurting their position unless carefully drafted. While it might seem beneficial to cover \"any other cause,\" Singapore courts have historically been reluctant to expand the scope of force majeure beyond the explicitly listed events, preferring a strict interpretation to maintain contractual certainty.\n\nRegarding recent precedents, the pandemic period (specifically the *Sembcorp Industries Ltd v. Kian Ming Construction Pte Ltd* and various retail lease disputes) highlighted that even with broad language, the burden of proof remains on the tenant to demonstrate that the event was truly beyond their control and that the clause was not intended to be a \"get out of jail free\" card for poor business decisions.\n\nHowever, if the clause explicitly includes \"government orders\" or \"pandemics,\" the tenant's position strengthens significantly, provided they can show they took reasonable steps to mitigate the loss. Without such specific inclusions, relying solely on vague \"catch-all\" phrases often leads to disputes where the landlord successfully argues the tenant should have foreseen the risk or failed to mitigate.",
